WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.754
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:09.900
What is the best CS
class you've ever taken?

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.520
I've taken a lot
of great classes.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.250
But honestly I think
I really enjoyed

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.990
601, which was the Intro
Computer Science course I

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:21.297
took at MIT my sophomore year.

00:00:21.297 --> 00:00:23.130
And I really liked it
because it was sort of

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.130
built around making a robot.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.290
And every week we would
build an additional thing

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.040
onto the robot.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.690
So the first week,
we would just make

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.340
it walk in a straight line.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.640
And the second week we would
try to make it turn corners.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.200
And the process of interactively
coding things and then

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.010
immediately seeing the robot
do what I just told it to do

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.575
was really fun for me.

00:00:46.575 --> 00:00:48.450
And probably one of the
reasons why I decided

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.730
to stick with computer science.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.923
Definitely algorithms
and data structures.

00:00:53.923 --> 00:00:55.590
I went to Harvard for
undergrad and they

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.777
had an algorithms class
that was notorious

00:00:58.777 --> 00:01:00.360
for being the hardest
computer science

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.760
class in the entire curriculum.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.860
People would shake when
they talked about it.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.577
And I remember being so
nervous and intimidated.

00:01:07.577 --> 00:01:09.160
But algorithms are
actually quite fun.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.490
There are hard
sometimes, and not

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.350
everything came
super easily to me.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.600
But I like the way that
you could sort of come up

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.637
with a little recipe
and how to solve this.

00:01:17.637 --> 00:01:20.220
And then analyze how well that
recipe works and whether or not

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.870
it actually gets
you what you want.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:27.750
I would say my favorite CS class
was Intro to Python and Intro

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.250
to Programming
because that's what

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.730
got me interested in a lot of
these algorithms and research

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:33.770
in the first place.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:37.190
It was definitely
Computer Vision

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:42.510
and was also one that I took
last semesters in CSAIL.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.670
It was particularly interesting
because I could see aspects

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.450
that I learned before.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.530
And in the way that
they are practical.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.670
And we even had to design
a camera for this class,

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:58.160
like a pinhole
camera, which made

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.270
it particularly interesting.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:04.930
My favorite computer science
class is probably Intro to AI.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:08.889
It was taught by Hod Lipson
at Cornell at the time.

00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.628
And it was really interesting
because I was really excited

00:02:11.628 --> 00:02:13.170
about all these
different things, AI.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.168
I want to know, how do you
make a game playing AI?

00:02:18.168 --> 00:02:19.960
How do you solve problems
in robotics where

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.380
they have to navigate a room?

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.800
After about two or three weeks,
I had coded up an algorithm

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.282
to solve--

00:02:27.282 --> 00:02:29.490
there's this board game
called Rush Hour where you're

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.200
trying to move cars
around and you're

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.455
trying to get this red
car out of a traffic jam.

00:02:33.455 --> 00:02:35.580
And I used to play this
game all the time as a kid.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.670
And after three weeks, I
had written an AI program

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:43.710
to do a search and basically
solve any level difficulty Rush

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.050
Hour problem in
less than a minute.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:49.740
And it was just it
was super empowering.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.670
And I saw all these applications
that I could use this for,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.833
for video games, for robotics.

00:02:54.833 --> 00:02:56.250
And that was just
really exciting,

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.530
about how quickly
I felt like I was

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.350
able to do really sophisticated
tasks after learning

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.340
that material.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.230
I took my Introduction
to Programming class

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:12.270
at UC Santa Cruz, which was
taught by Charlie McDowell.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:16.320
Was my first big experience
into programming.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:22.710
And I really enjoyed the class
because I learned so much

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.100
there, data structures,
programming flow,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.080
and some animation.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.440
So we used processing.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.390
And we make visual images move.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:39.330
And it was my first experience
really coding anything.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.220
And I really enjoyed that.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.910
So I would say probably
that was my favorite class.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

